X-ray Crystal Structure Determination. Diffraction-quality single crystals of 1a, 1b, and TPE- $\cdot d_{20}$ were mounted using mineral oil and epoxy on Kapton loops. Diffraction data ( $\varphi\mathrm{.}$ - and $\omega$ -scans) at 100, 298, and $373\mathrm{~K~}$ were collected on a Bruker-AXS X8 Kappa Duo diffractometer coupled to a Smart APEX II CCD detector with MoKα radiation $\overset{\prime}{\lambda}=0.71073\overset{\circ}{\mathrm{A}}$ ) from an $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{I}\mu\ensuremath{S}}$ -micro source. Absorption and polarization corrections were applied using SADABS.58 The structure was solved by direct methods using SHELXS and refined against $F^{2}$ on all data by full-matrix least-squares with SHELXL-97.59 All nonhydrogen atoms were refined anisotropically and were included in the model at geometrically calculated positions. The crystallographic data for TPE- $\cdot d_{20}$ and 1 are shown in Table S1.
$^2{\mathsf{H}}$ NMR Spectroscopy. Experiments were conducted on a homebuilt spectrometer (courtesy of Dr. Dave Ruben) operating at $61\mathrm{MHz}$ for $^2\mathrm{H}$ using a single-channel transmission line probe with $3.2~\mathrm{mm}$ coil. Spectra were obtained using a quadrupolar echo sequence60 with an 8- step phase cycling61 using a $\pi/2$ pulse of $2.0~\mu s$ and a delay of $30~\mu s$ between the two pulses. Phenyl ring motional dynamics were determined by simulations of the experimental $^2\mathrm{H}$ powder lineshapes using TURBOPOWDER.62
$^{13}C$ MAS NMR Spectroscopy. Experiments were performed at $16.4\mathrm{T}$ $\left(697.8~\mathrm{MHz},\mathrm{^{1}H}\right)$ using a home-built spectrometer (courtesy of Dr. Dave Ruben) and a $3.2~\mathrm{mm}$ Chemagnetics triple-channel magicangle spinning probe. Samples were ground using a mortar and pestle and packed in $3.2\ \mathrm{mm}\ \mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ rotors $\sim28\mu\mathrm{L}$ sample volume). Spectra were acquired at spinning frequencies of $^{10\mathrm{\kHz},}$ with 512−4096 coadded transients and recycle delays between 3 and $^{120\ s,}$ using either a Bloch decay or cross-polarization63 ( $\cdot\ensuremath{v_{\mathrm{rf}}}$ of $83\ \mathrm{kHz}$ for $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ and $^3\mathrm{C},\tau_{\mathrm{CP}}=2.0~\mathrm{ms})$ and two pulse phase modulation (TPPM) proton decoupling64 for naturally abundant $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ deuterated and protonated samples. $^{\mathrm{i}3}\mathrm{C}$ experiments were referenced to adamantane at 38.48 ppm relative to TMS.65
Computational Details. Calculations were performed using the ORCA 2.8 quantum chemistry program package from the development team at the University of Bonn.66 In all cases the LDA and GGA functionals employed were those of Perdew and Wang (PW-LDA, PW91).67 Calculations were performed using the TZV basis set for hydrogen, the $\mathrm{TZV(p)}$ basis set for main group atoms, and $\mathrm{TZV}(2\mathrm{pf})$ for zinc.68 Spin-restricted Kohn−Sham determinants have been chosen to describe the closed-shell wave functions, employing the RI approximation and the tight SCF convergence criteria provided by ORCA. Numerical frequency calculations were performed on the optimized structures when size would permit. The atoms-in-molecules analysis was performed using Xaim.69
Other Physical Measurements. TGA was performed on a TA Instruments Q500 thermogravimetric analyzer at a heating rate of 0.5 $^\mathrm{{{\circ}}}{\bf{C}}/\mathrm{{min}}$ under a nitrogen gas flow of $90\mathrm{\mL/min}$ . Infrared spectra were obtained on a PerkinElmer Spectrum 400 FT-IR/FT-FIR spectrometer equipped with a Pike Technologies GladiATR attenuated total reflectance accessory. Solution NMR spectra were collected on a Varian 300 or a Varian Inova-500 NMR spectrometer. $^2\mathrm{H}$ spectra were referenced to the natural abundance $^2\mathrm{H}$ peak in protonated solvents; $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ and $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ spectra were referenced to natural abundance $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ peaks and residual $^\mathrm{{\Delta^{-}I_{H}}}$ peaks of deuterated solvents, respectively. PXRD patterns for 1a and 1b were recorded on a Bruker Advance D8 diffractometer using nickel-filtered $_{\mathrm{Cu-K}\alpha}$ radiation ( $\lambda=$ 1.5418 Å), with accelerating voltage and current of $40\ \mathrm{kV}$ and $40\ \mathrm{mA},$ respectively. A PXRD pattern for 1c was collected at station 11-B at the Argonne National Laboratory using synchrotron radiation ( $\overset{\cdot}{\lambda}=$ 0.413073 Å). Samples for PXRD were prepared by placing a thin layer of the appropriate material on a silicon (510) crystal plate for 1a and 1b and by sealing 1c in a Kapton capillary.
